<article class="env-lemma" id="09WW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09WW">Lemma <span data-tag="09WW">4.35.14</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B} \to \mathcal{C}$ be functors between categories. If $\mathcal{A}$ is fibred in groupoids over $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{B}$ is fibred in groupoids over $\mathcal{C}$, then $\mathcal{A}$ is fibred in groupoids over $\mathcal{C}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      One can prove this directly from the definition. However, we will argue using the criterion of Lemma <a href="/tag/003V" data-tag="003V">4.35.2</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/09WV" data-tag="09WV">4.33.12</a> we see that $\mathcal{A}$ is fibred over $\mathcal{C}$. To finish the proof we show that the fibre category $\mathcal{A}_ U$ is a groupoid for $U$ in $\mathcal{C}$. Namely, if $x \to y$ is a morphism of $\mathcal{A}_ U$, then its image in $\mathcal{B}$ is an isomorphism as $\mathcal{B}_ U$ is a groupoid. But then $x \to y$ is an isomorphism, for example by Lemma <a href="/tag/02XL" data-tag="02XL">4.33.2</a> and the fact that every morphism of $\mathcal{A}$ is strongly $\mathcal{B}$-cartesian (see Lemma <a href="/tag/003V" data-tag="003V">4.35.2</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>